/* cascade notes:
there's two unit types. There's VOID and there's NOTHING.

all statements are expressions.
the last expression of a block is cascaded to it's parent.
the ';' symbol is a post-fix unary operator that takes in a VOID and returns a NOTHING.
declarations and assignments return VOID.
all types returned from expressions need to be caught (or returned from the procedure), except for the NOTHING type.
"catching something" here refers to either cascading or binding.
*/

/* argument notes:
all functions get exactly one argument.
if you want "more arguments" you can use a tuple destructure binding
*/

/* type notes:
ints can have any bit count. such as a i42 or u63.
binary operators are illegal on ints. instead there's a more flexible bool type. you could have a b1 for classic bool behavior, but could also use b8 or b64, or similar and cast ints and floats to it to fiddle with the bits and then cast back.
ints and floats will have fields. f32 would have a u23 mantissa field, a i8 exponent field and so on. i8 could have a b8 internally and so on to make it easy for people to deal with the layout of native types.

we allow user-defined operators between any 2 types.
if multiple operators have the same symbol with the same types, we compile-time error.
operators may be + - or other common ones, or unicode symbols like ⊕, ∘, ∃
*/

//example statements:
{
	let a = b;
	let mut a = b;
	a = b;
	(a, b) = c;
	let (a, b) = c;
	let mut (a, b) = c;
	let (mut a, b) = c;
	(let a, let mut b, c) = d;
	(let a, let mut b, (if c: d else e).*) = f;
	(if a: b else c).* = d;
	
	if a { ret; }
	if a: ret;
	if a { b } else c;
	if a: b else c;
	loop a;
	loop { a };
	loop { a } else b;

	while a { b; }
	while a: b;
	while a { b; } else c;
	while a: b else c;

	if a: while b: c else d //else should bind to while (always innermost nesting)

	for a in b { c; }
	for a in b: c;

	let a = (1); //paren
	let a = (); //tuple unit
	let a = (1,); //tuple one
	let a = (1, 2, 3); //tuple many
	let a = (1, 2, 3,); //tuple many trail
}

